// "No public path to a private document" — revised while building §5
// (monotone reachability). The original version (distinct + two
// relation-equality constraints, structurally mirroring tenant-isolation)
// turned out to demonstrate the *same* verdict tenant-isolation does, for
// the same reason: a relation-equality constraint pins one specific
// (object, relation) slot, but the terminal edge the search actually
// needs to satisfy the goal is a *different* slot the constraint never
// touches — so nothing stops the search from just adding that edge's
// tuple directly. See tenant-isolation.invariant's own comment, and
// docs/DECISIONS.md D-116, for the full reasoning; the short version is
// that a relation-equality constraint can rule a leak *in* (as evidence
// it's real) but essentially never rules one *out*, because it never
// forecloses an unrelated, unconstrained tuple satisfying the goal a
// completely different way.
//
// A structural guarantee that constraints alone can't produce is
// available a different way: type-level unreachability. This fixture's
// `o` is `private_document` (tools/schema-verifier/fixtures/schemas/
// tenancy.authz), whose `view` permission's entire rewrite closure —
// `owner: service_account` only — never once accepts `user` as a valid
// subject, directly or transitively. No constraints are needed at all:
// no tuple set, however constructed, can ever satisfy this goal, because
// the schema graph itself has no path from a `user`-typed variable to
// `private_document#view`. Expected verdict once §5 exists: HOLDS.
invariant no_public_path_to_private_document {
  s: user
  o: private_document

  goal: view(s, o)
}
